\section{From self-reference to the $L^2$ metric: the chain in fourteen steps}
\label{sec:selfref-l2-chain}

This section walks from the founding premise of the programme to the metric
structure of quantum state space in fourteen small steps, each stated so that a
reader unfamiliar with the underlying mathematics can follow the argument
rather than take it on authority. We are explicit about epistemic status
throughout: a step marked [\textsc{established}] rests on published
mathematics or physics; [\textsc{framework}] marks results proved or argued
within this programme but not yet independently vetted;
[\textsc{candidate}] marks a fenced suggestion, introduced with pointers
rather than claimed; [\textsc{open}] marks a step that cannot yet be proved by
anyone, ourselves included. There are exactly three open steps, and we collect
them at the end. The shape of the argument is simple: an observer embedded in
the world they describe must carry a blind spot; accounting for that blind
spot honestly forces complex numbers into the bookkeeping; operations
performed before any record exists must be thermodynamically free and hence
reversible; free and recordless operation leaves no way to forbid half-steps;
and a state space admitting continuous reversible motion between all pure
states can measure distance in only one way.
% TODO(John): optional footnote on novelty; requires Samson Abramsky's
% permission before citing a private communication. Disabled by default.
% \footnote{The connection between self-referential paradoxes and negative
% probability appears to be unexplored; S.~Abramsky (private communication,
% 2026) confirmed he had not previously considered it.}

\paragraph{Step 1 (\textsc{established}).}
\emph{A describer inside the description must have a blind spot.} Imagine a
perfectly detailed map of a room, hung inside that room. To be complete, the
map must show the map, which must show the map showing the map, and so on
without end. Or take Popper's version: build a machine that predicts its own
output one second from now, then wire it to do the opposite of whatever it
predicts. Whatever it predicts, it lies. This is one argument wearing many
costumes: Cantor's diagonal, G\"odel's incompleteness, Turing's halting
problem, and, in physics, Popper's predictor and Breuer's theorem that no
apparatus can perfectly measure a system containing itself
\autocite{cantor1891diagonal,goedel1931formal,turing1936computable,popper1950indeterminism1,popper1950indeterminism2,breuer1995selfmeasurement}.
Lawvere showed that these are instances of a single fixed-point theorem, and
Yanofsky's survey makes the common schema accessible
\autocite{lawvere1969diagonal,yanofsky2003universal}. No embedded observer can
hold a complete, consistent description of a whole that includes themselves.

\paragraph{Step 2 (\textsc{established}).}
\emph{Give the blind spot a bookkeeper.} Physics cannot forbid observers from
being embedded, since every observer is. The blind spot must therefore be
accounted for rather than wished away, and the accounting system for
ignorance already exists: Shannon's information theory
\autocite{shannon1948communication}. If an outcome is assigned probability
$p$, the surprise on its occurrence is the surprisal $s = -\log p$. Certain
events carry no surprise; events held impossible carry unbounded surprise.
Everything that follows is careful bookkeeping with $-\log p$.

\paragraph{Step 3 (\textsc{framework}).}
\emph{Self-ignorance breaks the ordinary books.} Try to keep ordinary books,
every entry a number in $[0,1]$, about oneself. The predictor of Step~1
returns: whatever probability the machine's books assign to the proposition
``I will output $0$'', the machine can read its own books and defeat the
entry. A complete self-assignment in $[0,1]$ is exactly the kind of total
self-description Step~1 forbids. Two honest escapes exist: forbid the
situation, which is Russell's route \autocite{russell1908types} and is
unavailable here because the observer really is embedded; or extend the
number system of the entries, allowing some internal bookkeeping entries to
go negative provided every entry corresponding to an actually observable
event remains an honest probability. Negative quasi-probabilities have a
respectable pedigree
\autocite{wigner1932quantum,dirac1942bakerian,feynman1987negative}, and
modern work shows that negativity is precisely equivalent to contextuality,
the impossibility of one globally consistent assignment
\autocite{abramskybrandenburger2011sheaf,spekkens2008negativity}. The
negativity--contextuality scaffolding is thus established. What is not in the
literature is the claim made here: that self-reference is the \emph{origin}
of the negativity, the signed entries being forced by the diagonal argument
of Step~1. This claim is original to the present programme.

\paragraph{Step 4 (\textsc{established} mathematics; physical identification \textsc{candidate}).}
\emph{Take the logarithm of a negative number.} The bookkeeper's rule is
$s = -\log p$, but some entries are now negative. On the real line
$\log(-\tfrac14)$ is undefined; yet the logarithm extends, analytically
continues, beyond the real line in exactly one natural way, and the key fact
is Euler's identity $e^{i\pi} = -1$, hence $\log(-1) = i\pi$. The surprisal
of a negative entry is therefore
\[
  s \;=\; -\log\lvert p\rvert \;-\; i\pi \, ,
\]
an ordinary real surprise about the magnitude plus an imaginary offset of
exactly $\pi$ recording the sign. The imaginary part of information is the
ledger line for the sign. One disclosure is owed: the continued logarithm is
many-valued, each circuit of the origin adding $2\pi i$, and the principal
branch is chosen here. The ambiguity is not a defect; the multiples of
$2\pi i$ reappear later as winding numbers.
% TODO(John): wire \ref to the winding-number appendix label once confirmed
% (appendix_winding_number_operator_2026-07-12.tex).

\paragraph{Step 5 (definitional, given Step 4).}
\emph{Exponentiate back and an amplitude appears.} Probabilities and
surprisals are inverses, $p = e^{-s}$. Feeding in the complex surprisal of
Step~4 yields a complex number $\psi = e^{-s}$, with $\lvert\psi\rvert$
carrying the magnitude of the old entry and $\arg\psi$ carrying the
accumulated multiples of $i\pi$. Complex amplitudes were not postulated;
self-ignorance was double-entered and the complex numbers entered through the
arithmetic.

\paragraph{Step 6 (\textsc{established} mathematics; closure premise \textsc{candidate}, half-open).}
\emph{The continuation is a package deal.} Analytic continuation does not
merely define $\log(-1)$; it defines $z^{t} = e^{t\log z}$ for every real
$t$. Accepting $\log(-1) = i\pi$ means accepting
$(-1)^{1/2} = e^{i\pi/2} = i$, and $(-1)^{1/3}$, and $(-1)^{0.1387}$: one
cannot buy the sign flip without buying the entire continuous dial of phases,
the circle $U(1)$. Nor is there a halfway house, since no finite set of
phases survives the analytic root: the principal square root of a primitive
$k$-th root of unity has order $2k$ and escapes the set. Hence \emph{if} the
phases the formalism attains are closed under the roots the formalism itself
defines, the attained phase group is either trivial, the classical case
already excluded by Step~3, or the full circle. That conditional is a
premise, not yet a theorem; it is a phase-group instance of the divisibility
premise discussed at Step~9, and is recorded as a candidate.

\paragraph{Step 7 (\textsc{established}).}
\emph{What a transformation costs.} Landauer showed that \emph{erasing} one
bit of information carries an unavoidable price of $kT\ln 2$ paid to the
environment; Bennett showed the converse, that logically reversible
operations, those discarding nothing, can in principle be performed at zero
cost \autocite{landauer1961irreversibility,bennett1973logical}. The price tag
attaches to forgetting, never to doing, and the principle has been confirmed
experimentally \autocite{berut2012landauer}.

\paragraph{Step 8 (\textsc{framework}).}
\emph{The FANOUT boundary: before the first copy, everything is free.} Call
FANOUT the moment information is first copied, written into a record that
could in principle be read. The criterion central to this programme is that
this copying moment, not any collapse postulate, is the boundary between the
pre-measurement and measurement regimes; its shadow in the external
literature is the no-cloning theorem \autocite{wootterszurek1982cloning}.
Combining with Step~7: before FANOUT nothing has been written anywhere, so
nothing can require erasing, so no transformation can be charged. Pre-FANOUT
transformations are thermodynamically free, and must therefore be reversible,
because an irreversible step is precisely one that discards a distinction,
and discarding is the one thing that costs. Even the cancellation of signed
entries against one another, which will grow up to be interference, is free:
cancellation before FANOUT erases no record, there being no record to erase.

\paragraph{Step 9 (\textsc{open}: the central unproved step).}
\emph{Free and recordless means no step is special.} Aaronson's intuition
runs: if an operation can be applied for one second, it ought to make sense
to apply it for half a second \autocite{aaronson2013democritus}. The present
framework supplies a reason to believe this rather than merely find it
plausible. Suppose that pre-FANOUT there were a smallest permitted unit of
transformation, a rule of whole steps only. Then partway through a step there
would have to be a fact of the matter about how much had been applied, and a
fact is a distinguished mark, a record; but pre-FANOUT is precisely the
regime with no records. A minimal step size is a mark the recordless regime
has nowhere to keep, so every attainable transformation should be
\emph{divisible}: performable in halves, thirds, $n$-ths, for every $n$. We
state plainly that this step is not yet a theorem, ours or anyone's, and that
a standing counterexample enforces honesty: Spekkens' toy and epistricted
theories are likewise built on ignorance, yet their transformation groups are
finite, their ignorance rationed in indivisible whole bits
\autocite{spekkens2007toy,spekkens2016quasi}. Ignorance alone therefore does
not force divisibility; what must be shown is that \emph{self-referential}
ignorance, arriving with the continuous dial of Step~6 attached, differs in
kind from rationed ignorance. This is the programme's open divisibility
premise.
% TODO(John): wire \ref to the continuity appendix open-problem label (P2')
% once the label name is confirmed.

\paragraph{Step 10 (closure standard; substrate \textsc{open}).}
\emph{Limits of the allowed are allowed.} One further ingredient appears
mild: if a sequence of permitted transformations converges to a limiting
transformation, no experiment could certify the limit's absence, each stage
being indistinguishable from something permitted, so operationally the limit
is permitted too, and the set of attainable transformations is closed. The
move is standard in the reconstruction literature, but it quietly leans on
the scalars beneath forming a continuum: talk of convergence presupposes the
real numbers rather than, say, the rationals or the $p$-adics, and that
presupposition is itself an unproved layer of the programme. Khrennikov's
$p$-adic probability theory is the standing rival, and $\mathbb{Q}_p$ is
totally disconnected, admitting no continuous dial at all
\autocite{khrennikov1994padic}.

\paragraph{Step 11 (\textsc{established}).}
\emph{Divisible and closed makes a smoothly connected group.} Three classical
facts carry this step. The Cartan--von~Neumann closed subgroup theorem states
that a closed set of matrices closed under multiplication and inversion is
automatically a smooth object, a Lie group \autocite{lee2013smooth}. Compact
Lie groups have finitely many connected components. And by Lagrange's
observation, in a finite group of order $m$ only the identity is an $m$-th
power; so if an element possesses roots of every order, as Step~9 provides,
its component label must be the identity label, and divisibility forces every
element into the component of the identity. The attained transformations
therefore form a connected, continuous group. Every ingredient is
nineteenth-to-twentieth-century mathematics, and the step is theorem-grade
given Steps~9 and~10.

\paragraph{Step 12 (orbit argument \textsc{established}; homogeneity premise \textsc{framework}).}
\emph{No state is special, so the group reaches everything.} The founding
axiom of the programme, inside/outside equivalence, entails that physical law
cannot privilege any particular pure state, an embedded observer possessing
no absolute landmark against which to locate their own state; the symmetry
group must therefore carry any pure state to any other, acting transitively.
A connected group acting transitively yields, between any two pure states, a
continuous path of reversible transformations. Stated in Hardy's words this
is exactly his fifth axiom, the one postulated in the original
five-axiom reconstruction and still retained as a postulate in Schack's
four-axiom Bayesian variant \autocite{hardy2001,schack2003}. Here it has
become a conclusion, resting on the steps flagged above.

\paragraph{Step 13 (\textsc{established}; the record-theoretic gloss is a rhyme).}
\emph{Only one distance survives continuous motion.} A distance between
states, to mean anything, must be unchanged by the free reversible
operations; otherwise costless, recordless motion could alter how
distinguishable two states are, and a change in distinguishability is itself
a record that nothing paid for. The metric must therefore be invariant under
the whole connected group of Step~12. Consider the natural candidates,
distances built from $\sum_k \lvert \cdot \rvert^{p}$, and picture the unit
ball of each: for $p=1$ a diamond, for $p=\infty$ a box, for $p=2$ a round
sphere. A diamond or a box has corners, and corners are distinguished
directions, marks, exactly what the recordless regime cannot possess. The
theorem beneath the picture is due to Banach and Lamperti
\autocite{banach1932operations,lamperti1958isometries}: for every
$p \neq 2$ the norm-preserving linear maps are exactly the monomial ones,
permutations dressed with phases, a rigid set of moves admitting no
continuous path between distinct configurations. Continuous transitive motion
is incompatible with every exponent except one; the sphere is the only
cornerless ball, and $p=2$ is the only exponent whose symmetries can flow.
Hence the $L^2$ metric. One precision, so that nothing is oversold:
continuity of this kind selects the \emph{exponent}, not the field. Real
$L^2$ passes the transformation test, $SO(N)$ being connected and transitive
on the sphere; the complex field is bought separately, by the phase argument
of Step~6 at the amplitude level and by local tomography at the composite
level.

\paragraph{Step 14 (\textsc{established}, modulo citation checks).}
\emph{Two independent roads confirm the same exponent.} From the group
directly: a connected group transitive on pure states, with the phase circle
of Step~6 as fibre, makes the pure-state space complex projective space
$\mathbb{CP}^{K-1}$, which admits exactly one invariant metric up to overall
scale, the Fubini--Study metric, whose linear lift is the $L^2$ inner
product \autocite{bengtssonzyczkowski2017geometry}. From statistics, with no
groups at all: \v{C}encov proved that the unique metric on classical
probability distributions respecting coarse-graining is the Fisher--Rao
metric \autocite{cencov1982statistical,rao1945information}, and in the
coordinates $x_k = \sqrt{p_k}$ the Fisher--Rao metric \emph{is} the round
$L^2$ metric on the positive orthant of the sphere. The square root of
probability is thus a natural statistical coordinate even classically;
Wootters showed that quantum distinguishability distance is exactly the
corresponding angle \autocite{wootters1981distance}, and Petz and
Petz--Sud\'ar showed that although mixed states carry a whole family of
monotone metrics, on pure states every member of the family collapses to
Fubini--Study \autocite{petz1996monotone,petzsudar1996geometries}.
Transformation continuity selects the exponent; value continuity
independently confirms it. What the statistical road can never supply is the
passage from the positive orthant to the whole sphere, the linear structure,
phases and superposition: that is entirely transformation-bought.

\paragraph{The ledger of open steps.}
Fully load-bearing on published mathematics and physics: Steps 1, 2, the
mathematics of Step~4, and Steps 7, 11, 13 and 14. Proved or argued within
this programme but not independently vetted: Step~3, the origin of negativity
in self-reference; Step~8, the FANOUT boundary; the homogeneity premise of
Step~12; and the physical identifications in Steps 4 and 5. Genuinely open,
provable by no one yet: Step~9, that recordlessness forces divisibility,
with the Spekkens foil standing guard over exactly where the proof must
bite; the substrate of Step~10, why the real continuum underlies the scalars
at all; and, half-open, the closure premise inside Step~6, whose mathematics
is trivial and whose physical content is not. The chain is thus a genuine
derivation with exactly three named gaps, each gap a precise mathematical
statement rather than a hope.

